WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.920
What's it like to have your life story
told on the big screen?

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:11.300
Sir when you said—I say a lot of things, I'm a politician. So what you did so that low-income students

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:16.760
could go to private schools? Well I want to do that here. You have a better chance of winning the lottery

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:22.560
than getting that done. The new movie, Miss Virginia, tells the unlikely story of Virginia Walden Ford,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:28.280
who as a single mother in Washington D.C. in the early 2000's fought to create a federally funded

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:35.320
private school voucher program that would allow poor kids, including her son, to escape failing public schools.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:41.480
Against long odds and institutional hostility,  she succeeded, and is played with fiery passion

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:47.020
by "Orange is the New Black" Uzo Abduba. Every time she says, "my name is Virginia Walden,"

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:54.320
I freak out. I just go, "Oh my gosh she's using my name!" That kind of silliness. So I'm...it's surreal.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:01:01.460
I never ever thought that anybody would even notice our fight. So it also is humbling.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:08.020
You know, I mean we were a ragtag group of parents going up on the hill talking to members of Congress

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:13.780
to help save our kids. And so it's it's surreal and unreal to me. What in your experience

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:22.280
with the D.C. Public Schools and your son what was the essential thing that they were failing him by?

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:26.820
I think he was just one of those kids who's just gonna fall through the cracks you know if

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:33.160
somebody didn't intervene. I remember going to school one day to talk to a teacher about a class that he

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:38.880
had said he was having trouble with and she didn't even know who he was. She had 40 kids in her class.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:43.840
Walden Ford's own backstory as a student is both harrowing and inspirational.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.420
She was among the early waves of black kids that integrated public schools in Little Rock Arkansas

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:53.920
in the late 1950s, an experience that informed her actions as a parent.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.280
I remember my dad saying you're going to Central. And I'm like oh no I'm not.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:06.680
I'm going to the black high school. And he told me you have a responsibility to go to Central and do well,

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:14.600
because you have younger siblings and the world will look at you based on the fact that you

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:22.100
paved the way for for your younger siblings. And even at 14, I was 14, I took that really seriously.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:30.360
And I really believe my parents were people that serve the community and I remember thinking

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:36.980
that's someone—I want to make a difference. So at 14 I knew that I was going to

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.800
do things that would make a difference, that I was going to serve my community.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:48.040
But it was based on my parents, my grandfather was a slave who bought his brothers out of slavery,

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:56.460
and ran a bakery in Little Rock. That's how he raised the money. And in knowing that I had that kind of

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:05.140
legacy in my family certainly impacted who I am and who my sisters are. But service was a big deal.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:11.040
When Daddy got the job at Little Rock's School District, he was the first black assistant superintendent

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.120
for Little Rock's School District. You know the Klan burned a cross in our yard,

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:22.220
they threw rocks through our windows, and I remember my dad saying, you know, don't be mad,

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:23.580
change the world.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:31.780
There's a bit of irony in that as a child as a young person you fought for access to the public schools, as a parent

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:38.520
you were fighting for the right of exit from the public school system. What's the consistent throughline

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:45.070
in those actions? In both actions we're fighting for quality education. During civil rights movement,

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:52.860
when we were trying to get into public schools it was not to get in the building, it was to get

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:58.960
what was in the building. We were taught with books there were oftentimes out-of-date.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.340
Science labs where 20 kids would use one microscope. Or,

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:13.080
those kinds of things, that really keep kids from getting what they really need. Separate but equal was a lie.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:20.980
Exactly. When schools started changing and deteriorating, I remember saying this is not acceptable.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:26.500
Actually I send speeches all the time to African Americans, I know why you mad about

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:31.820
it coming out of public schools and stuff I know how you felt. I was there.

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:37.400
But, do you really want our kids to stay in bad schools? You know because we fought

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:44.520
60 years ago to get in another building? That's not what we want. So you gotta get  it in your head,

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:52.380
this is something different. I remember telling Ted Kennedy one time, your brothers fought for us

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:56.900
to get into traditional public schools, and why are you fighting against us

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:07.280
getting out of these same schools because they're bad? Now those up on the hill, they said we would fail.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:15.000
They say that we all don't care about our
kids. But they could not be more wrong.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:21.440
One of the most persistent and paternalistic criticisms of parental empowerment in education is that

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:25.380
poor, uneducated parents simply don't have the background or the intelligence

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:30.240
to make informed decisions for their kids.
Walden Ford is having none of that.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.120
Every time we went to a meeting in the
communities that we were serving,

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:40.040
which were low income communities in Washington, I would ask Q&amp;A

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:45.020
you know we'd do a Q&amp;A, and I would say what do you want for your child? Low-income parents

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:53.160
will come there with lists of what they wanted for their children. And they were articulate and passionate,

00:05:53.160 --> 00:06:01.280
and it was amazing to hear them speak about what their desires were,

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:08.740
and what they would hope for their children if their children an opportunity to go to a school

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:15.920
that served them well. What's the basic metric of a successful school in a choice environment for you?

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:21.160
When you walk into a school and kids are smiling, and they're engaged, and they're safe,

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:28.180
and parents are involved, that's a key to a successful school. And they have the equipment and library.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:34.140
So that kind of stuff is more important than test scores or—you know I think you do have to test kids.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:39.460
I mean I think you have to determine where they are and what they need to learn,

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:46.860
but I think they you can't define their whole existence by test scores. I think there are a lot of things that

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:52.820
go along with that. With the D.C. Opportunity Scholarship Program, in their initial years

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:59.520
the fact that kids were safe and their parents were happy, and they were graduating at

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:07.420
20% above the rate of D.C. Public Schools that was way more important than test scores.

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:12.140
They were going on. You know people never did understand it when our kids went into those schools

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:18.480
they were behind. So they had to catch up. And so test scores in the early years for some time are just not

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:27.500
correct. And those same kids were —William graduated, he graduated valedictorian. But in D.C. public school

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:33.420
his test scores were three or four levels below wherever whatever grade he was in.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:37.720
I used to have to fight him to go to school I dropped him off at school he'd leave.

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:43.180
I would drive away and he'd just be gone. I get a call from school saying he wasn't there.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:51.420
But when he was moved to a private school he liked it, he liked going there, he would jump up

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:55.660
and get ready for school and I asked him why are you acting like that?

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:59.240
Okay I'm waiting for the other shoe to drop. When are you gonna do something silly?

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:06.160
And he'd say Mama—and thirteen year olds don't talk like this, but he said Mama for the first time in my life

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:10.540
I believe that others care about me learning other than you.

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:15.520
Walden Ford is adamant that partisan politics not get in the way of helping children.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:20.220
She acknowledges that having Donald Trump support school choice is a good thing for the movement,

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:25.220
even as it possibly makes reform harder to sell among blacks and Latinos.

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:29.380
She's equally dismayed that none of the leading Democratic candidates for president

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:31.380
has embraced school choice.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:36.160
She's especially troubled by senator Cory Booker who as mayor of Newark, New Jersey

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:41.580
was a strong proponent of education reform. I am constantly disappointed

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:46.240
every time I read something, especially from Corey, because I know him well.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:53.020
And I know what a big supporter he was. What's so easy to say, and I'm a Democrat, and it's so easy for us to say,

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:57.900
they just need more money. I mean that is that's the most wrongheaded way

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:01.780
of thinking about education these days. It's not just about more money,

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:06.560
it's about a result, it's about achievement, it's about how we're delivering that education,

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:13.380
and the choices we're giving our parents. To me it's disappointing and that's why I think this is the best time

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:21.980
ever for this film to come out. Because what I'm seeing is a lot of people that are supporting

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:27.920
people that oppose school choice to get elected. And that really concerns me.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:32.100
If we don't figure out how to get everybody on the same page

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.940
about educating children, this country is going to be a mess anyway.

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:40.120
Now poor kids have just as much of a right to learn as rich ones,

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:45.700
However the 2020 election plays out or even how the school choice movement evolves,

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:50.060
Walden Ford is emphatic that "Miss Virginia," the movie version of her life,

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:54.320
has a message that needs to be clearly understood and embraced.

00:09:54.320 --> 00:10:03.120
You know, you have every right to look at your child and determine where he will best be taught,

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:07.840
and that's what I want parents to take away. You don't have to sit back and just take it.

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:16.800
At the end of the day this movie certainly is my story but it's any parent's story. It's every parent's story,

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:21.280
who ever fought for their children, so it honors the parents.

